WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.080
<v The Announcer>Previously, on LAiDIES: our heroine
learned that talking to AI isn't coding

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.520
<v The Announcer>— it's delegation.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.882
<v The Announcer>She stopped typing three vague words and
hoping, and started briefing the machine

00:00:13.882 --> 00:00:19.680
<v The Announcer>like a smart new hire — David Rose
specific — and got back something she'd
actually send.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:26.169
<v The Announcer>And on this episode: she gets an answer
with full Regina George confidence,

00:00:26.169 --> 00:00:32.380
<v The Announcer>almost uses it... and then notices one
tiny detail tugging at the corner of the
story.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.660
<v The Announcer>This is Episode Three: The Burn Book
Problem.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.620
<v The Heroine>It's a Thursday afternoon, and the
machine just handed me something that
looks glorious.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:50.700
<v The Heroine>I'd asked one of the AI tools to turn
four days of messy meeting notes into a
clean client update.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:58.340
<v The Heroine>Nine seconds later, there it was —
structured, calm, completely sure of
itself.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.614
<v The Heroine>It even opened with "per our
discussion," which I would never, but

00:01:03.614 --> 00:01:09.280
<v The Heroine>somehow coming from the machine it read
as competent instead of
passive-aggressive.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.260
<v The Heroine>I was two clicks from sending.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.820
<v The Heroine>And then one sentence caught my eye.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.660
<v The Heroine>The draft said the client had "approved"
a July rollout.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.420
<v The Heroine>Except — I was in that meeting.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.920
<v The Heroine>Nobody approved anything.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:34.200
<v The Heroine>What we actually said was "July could
work, if procurement clears by Friday."

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.200
<v The Heroine>The machine took a maybe... and gave it
a lanyard.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.704
<v The Heroine>And I couldn't help but wonder... if a
paragraph can sound that sure of itself

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:49.827
<v The Heroine>— hair done, makeup done, "per our
discussion" and everything — how am I

00:01:49.827 --> 00:01:54.740
<v The Heroine>supposed to catch the one line in it
that's quietly, completely wrong.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.764
<v The Heroine>Welcome back to LAiDIES — where smart,
busy women learn AI one Wednesday at a

00:02:00.764 --> 00:02:03.820
<v The Heroine>time, from a little internet town called
SUNNYVAiLE.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.764
<v The Heroine>Last week, David Rose taught us to say
what we actually want — fold in the

00:02:10.764 --> 00:02:12.680
<v The Heroine>cheese, but for your inbox.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:19.220
<v The Heroine>This week, Elle Woods teaches us what to
check before we believe what comes back.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.900
<v The Heroine>Because here's what nobody warns you
about when you start: AI can sound
right.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.720
<v The Heroine>And sounding right is not the same thing
as being right.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.020
<v The Heroine>Before your name goes on it, you need to
know which kind of answer you're
holding.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.580
<v The Heroine>So I did what this town has taught me —
I shut the laptop and took it to Main
Street.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:48.315
<v The Heroine>To the NewsStand on Main, whose job is
separating what actually happened from

00:02:48.315 --> 00:02:50.180
<v The Heroine>what just sounds like it did.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:57.883
<v The Heroine>No doom, no hype, no "a source close to
the situation." And standing there with

00:02:57.883 --> 00:03:03.700
<v The Heroine>the real headlines, it landed: my draft
didn't need a better writer.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.669
<v The Heroine>It needed someone at the news desk,
holding every confident line up to the

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:11.900
<v The Heroine>light — says who? based on what?

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.180
<v The Heroine>The machine had handed me a front page.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.060
<v The Heroine>My job was to fact-check it before it
ran under my byline.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.440
<v The Heroine>Let me tell you about the Burn Book.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.140
<v The Heroine>It didn't work because it was true —
none of it was, and nobody checked.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:35.120
<v The Heroine>It worked because it had social
authority: a rumor, a grudge, a wild

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.895
<v The Heroine>guess, and something fully unhinged, all
in the same handwriting, with the same

00:03:40.895 --> 00:03:43.060
<v The Heroine>devastating teenage certainty.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:50.391
<v The Heroine>AI writes in that same hand — that same
certainty — but here's the twist: it

00:03:50.391 --> 00:03:52.400
<v The Heroine>doesn't only write rumors.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.646
<v The Heroine>It'll take a real source, an old source,
a similar-but-not-this source, and an

00:03:58.646 --> 00:04:04.160
<v The Heroine>assumption it made because the pattern
looked familiar, and hand you one smooth

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.400
<v The Heroine>paragraph like everyone in it belongs
together.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:13.788
<v The Heroine>That's the Burn Book Problem: an
official court record and an entry in

00:04:13.788 --> 00:04:19.202
<v The Heroine>the Burn Book come out in that exact
same handwriting — same confidence, same

00:04:19.202 --> 00:04:23.020
<v The Heroine>finish — you can't tell which is which
until you check.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:29.900
<v The Heroine>Take the Bethany Byrd moment from Mean
Girls — a perfect, tiny sourcing
disaster.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:36.426
<v The Heroine>Somebody ( Regina) writes in the Burn
Book that Bethany must be lying about

00:04:36.426 --> 00:04:40.380
<v The Heroine>being a virgin — because she buys
super-jumbo tampons.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.720
<v The Heroine>One box of tampons, and boom — a
verdict, filed.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:52.342
<v The Heroine>Then the actual explanation walks in,
much less scandalous and much more

00:04:52.342 --> 00:04:58.860
<v The Heroine>specific: she's just got a heavy flow
and a wide set.....well you know the
rest...

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:06.480
<v The Heroine>That was never evidence. It's a clue in
a Claire's headband, sprinting straight
to a conclusion.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:11.380
<v The Heroine>One data point. No context. Enormous
conclusion.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:17.760
<v The Heroine>So the question is never "can I use AI?"
Yes. Use it.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.620
<v The Heroine>We are not here to churn butter by
candlelight.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.738
<v The Heroine>The question is: which parts is it just
drafting for you — and which parts are

00:05:26.738 --> 00:05:30.260
<v The Heroine>claims that need receipts before they
borrow your name?

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.443
<v The Heroine>Now, a few wrong answers are easy to
spot — the product that doesn't exist,

00:05:36.443 --> 00:05:38.820
<v The Heroine>the answer that argues with itself.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.100
<v The Heroine>But the fake citation?

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:43.420
<v The Heroine>That one's dressed to pass.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:49.190
<v The Heroine>It looks exactly like a real source —
cited with the confidence of "my

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:55.000
<v The Heroine>boyfriend goes to another school" —
right up until you click it and it goes
nowhere.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:58.840
<v The Heroine>And the sneakiest one isn't fake at all.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.080
<v The Heroine>It's real information in the wrong
place.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:08.570
<v The Heroine>It brought the wrong ID and somehow made
it past the door. A U.S.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.650
<v The Heroine>HR answer in a Canadian workplace.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.050
<v The Heroine>Last year's pricing page wearing this
year's lip gloss.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:23.487
<v The Heroine>"We talked about it" quietly promoted to
"we decided." A policy answer that's

00:06:23.487 --> 00:06:28.490
<v The Heroine>technically true, except the exception
is the part that matters.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:34.911
<v The Heroine>That's the moment you stand up in the
back, in your blue hoodie, strings

00:06:34.911 --> 00:06:40.990
<v The Heroine>pulled tight, and your oversized
sunglasses, and you shout: "SHE DOESN'T
EVEN GO HERE!"

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.330
<v The Heroine>It's not just a great line.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.750
<v The Heroine>It's a quality-control standard.

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:50.870
<v The Heroine>And this is where Elle Woods becomes the
patron saint of AI verification.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:56.951
<v The Heroine>Not because she makes "being thorough"
sound corporate — because she spots the

00:06:56.951 --> 00:07:02.590
<v The Heroine>one detail everyone else treated like
lip gloss and realizes it's holding up
the alibi.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:09.290
<v The Heroine>Chutney says she was in the shower,
right after getting a perm. Elle asks
again.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.050
<v The Heroine>Chutney gives the same story.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:17.005
<v The Heroine>The room rolls its eyes, because it
sounds like Elle is making haircare

00:07:17.005 --> 00:07:19.510
<v The Heroine>small talk in the middle of a murder
trial.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.330
<v The Heroine>But Elle isn't checking whether Chutney
can repeat herself.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.450
<v The Heroine>She's waiting for the detail that
doesn't fit.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:32.754
<v The Heroine>And if you know one thing about perms,
you know you do not wash a fresh one —

00:07:32.754 --> 00:07:38.590
<v The Heroine>not unless you're trying to destroy your
alibi and your hair in the same
afternoon.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:45.670
<v The Heroine>One tiny beauty-world rule nobody took
seriously... and the story falls apart.

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.870
<v The Heroine>With AI, that's the job.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:53.850
<v The Heroine>Don't ask "does this sound smart?"
Chutney sounded smart.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.070
<v The Heroine>She sounded smart three times.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:01.590
<v The Heroine>Ask: what's the one detail that can't
survive contact with the timeline?

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:06.190
<v The Heroine>If it says a policy changed — what date
proves it?

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:10.510
<v The Heroine>If it says a quote came from a person —
where's the quote?

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:17.570
<v The Heroine>If it says a number went up — from what,
over what timeframe, according to whom?

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:20.510
<v The Heroine>Do not be Chutney on the stand.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.890
<v The Heroine>Be Elle with the timeline.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:28.050
<v The Heroine>So before an answer leaves your laptop,
sort it into three piles.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:34.590
<v The Heroine>A draft is wording, structure, a
brainstorm, a summary — that can be
fast.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:41.651
<v The Heroine>A claim is a name, a date, a number, a
quote, a source, anything legal, HR,

00:08:41.651 --> 00:08:44.830
<v The Heroine>privacy, or money — that needs checking.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:52.110
<v The Heroine>And a receipt is the thing you can
actually open, name, or point to if
someone asks.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:54.910
<v The Heroine>A draft is an outfit.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:58.850
<v The Heroine>A claim is an alibi. Dress accordingly.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:06.470
<v The Heroine>And before this sounds like a second job
— remember who you are here. You're
Elle.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:11.740
<v The Heroine>You're the one who stands up in that
courtroom, finds the detail that doesn't

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.510
<v The Heroine>hold up, and wins with her name on the
case.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:16.950
<v The Heroine>Nobody hands you that part.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:23.310
<v The Heroine>But here's what Elle didn't have: a law
clerk who already did the reading.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:28.886
<v The Heroine>The machine spent the weekend in the
library so you didn't — it did the

00:09:28.886 --> 00:09:30.770
<v The Heroine>research, it drafted the brief.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:37.943
<v The Heroine>You still read it, you still check the
citations, you still walk in and own it

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:42.390
<v The Heroine>— because when the judge looks up, she's
looking at you.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.230
<v The Heroine>That's not extra work.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:50.370
<v The Heroine>The grunt work got done for you; the
judgment stayed yours.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:57.706
<v The Heroine>And please — asking AI "are you sure?"
is like asking Regina George whether the

00:09:57.706 --> 00:10:03.270
<v The Heroine>Burn Book is peer reviewed. Bold choice.
Limited value.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:08.839
<v The Heroine>Sometimes it catches the mistake,
corrects itself, and we all briefly

00:10:08.839 --> 00:10:10.370
<v The Heroine>believe in personal growth.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:16.738
<v The Heroine>And sometimes it just hands you the same
wrong answer in the same questionable

00:10:16.738 --> 00:10:22.295
<v The Heroine>outfit consisting of two popped-collar
polos, one over the other (of course),

00:10:22.295 --> 00:10:27.430
<v The Heroine>but this time with the shirt order
switched. That's not verification.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:32.697
<v The Heroine>That's Chutney repeating the alibi in
the same questionable outfit, just with

00:10:32.697 --> 00:10:34.610
<v The Heroine>a slightly different colour combo.

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:40.380
<v The Heroine>Now — before someone in the back adjusts
a butterfly clip and says, "but the

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.670
<v The Heroine>tools are getting better" — yes. They
are.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:49.382
<v The Heroine>The newer ones can search, cite, read
documents you hand them, and sometimes

00:10:49.382 --> 00:10:50.970
<v The Heroine>flag when they're unsure.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.690
<v The Heroine>That's real, and it helps.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:56.690
<v The Heroine>But better is not solved.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:03.383
<v The Heroine>A 2026 Nature paper put a name to why:
the way we grade these models rewards a

00:11:03.383 --> 00:11:08.070
<v The Heroine>confident guess over an honest "I don't
know" — so they guess.

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:15.003
<v The Heroine>Stanford's 2026 AI Index found something
scarier for our purposes: tell a top

00:11:15.003 --> 00:11:21.350
<v The Heroine>model something false that you seem to
believe, and it'll often just agree with
you.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:26.902
<v The Heroine>And KPMG — one of the Big Four
accounting firms, the serious-suit

00:11:26.902 --> 00:11:32.659
<v The Heroine>people — had to pull an AI report after
someone checked its sources and found

00:11:32.659 --> 00:11:37.250
<v The Heroine>forty of the forty-five were made up.
Forty of forty-five.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:41.890
<v The Heroine>Big Four. Tiny receipt drawer.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:45.510
<v The Heroine>So attach the sources, absolutely.

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:51.876
<v The Heroine>Just don't assume "sources attached"
means "sources checked." Sometimes it's

00:11:51.876 --> 00:11:56.110
<v The Heroine>still Chutney, handing you a folder in
her own handwriting.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:02.152
<v The Heroine>And the good news is, the serious
guidance — from OpenAI, Anthropic,

00:12:02.152 --> 00:12:07.890
<v The Heroine>Google, Stanford — all points the same
way, and it comes down to three moves.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:11.350
<v The Heroine>Move one: give her the source.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:17.895
<v The Heroine>Don't ask the machine what it remembers
— paste the real document, the actual

00:12:17.895 --> 00:12:24.410
<v The Heroine>policy, this year's pricing page; when
you need something current, turn on its
search mode.

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:32.630
<v The Heroine>Then one line: "answer only from what I
just gave you." Elle doesn't argue from
memory.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.930
<v The Heroine>She walks in with the file.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:42.917
<v The Heroine>Move two: let her say "I don't know."
Add the sentence most people never think

00:12:42.917 --> 00:12:49.599
<v The Heroine>to add — "if it's not in there, say so;
don't fill the gap." That one line is

00:12:49.599 --> 00:12:54.310
<v The Heroine>what turns a confident guess back into
an honest blank.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:59.310
<v The Heroine>Move three: make her show the line by
asking:

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.850
<v The Heroine>"Quote the exact sentence you got that
from."

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:08.200
<v The Heroine>If she can't point to it, that claim
showed up at Spring Fling with no

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.850
<v The Heroine>student ID — and it doesn't get to
speak.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:16.190
<v The Heroine>One rule over all three: no invented
receipts.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:20.110
<v The Heroine>No made-up links, dates, quotes, or
numbers.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:23.490
<v The Heroine>Watch it work on my Thursday disaster.

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:29.201
<v The Heroine>Instead of "clean up these notes," I
pasted the notes and said: summarize

00:13:29.201 --> 00:13:33.916
<v The Heroine>only what's here, mark anything we
didn't actually decide as "pending," and

00:13:33.916 --> 00:13:36.150
<v The Heroine>show me the line behind every claim.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:43.810
<v The Heroine>Same nine seconds — no phantom July
approval, because I never left a gap for
the machine to fill.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:51.790
<v The Heroine>That's Prompt Like Elle: hand her the
file, let her say "I don't know," make
her show the line.

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:57.790
<v The Heroine>Which brings me to my favourite part of
every episode — the cocktail party
explanation.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:03.977
<v The Heroine>The answer you'll give the next time
someone at happy hour puts down her

00:14:03.977 --> 00:14:10.110
<v The Heroine>drink and asks, "wait — why does it just
make things up?" Here it is: it's not
lying.

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:13.470
<v The Heroine>Lying takes intent. It's guessing.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:18.839
<v The Heroine>When the machine doesn't know, it
doesn't stop — it reaches for the most

00:14:18.839 --> 00:14:22.310
<v The Heroine>plausible-sounding thing and says it
with its whole chest.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.010
<v The Heroine>It's your most confident friend.

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:29.730
<v The Heroine>The one who will answer any question,
whether or not she actually knows.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.090
<v The Heroine>The fix was never to catch her in a lie.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:37.630
<v The Heroine>It's to ask for the receipt before you
repeat her in a meeting.

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:41.210
<v The Heroine>And... that's the episode.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:43.650
<v The Heroine>Now — your try-on.

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:49.090
<v The Heroine>Ten minutes, not homework, and
everything you need lives at laidies.ai.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:54.990
<v The Heroine>That's "LAiDIES" spelled with an i in
the middle: L, A, i, D, I, E, S.

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:59.868
<v The Heroine>And if you'd rather read than listen,
the full episode's written up there too

00:14:59.868 --> 00:15:02.910
<v The Heroine>— every source laid out where you can
check it yourself.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:08.703
<v The Heroine>And the exact Prompt Like Elle wording —
the three moves, copy-paste ready — is

00:15:08.703 --> 00:15:10.230
<v The Heroine>waiting in your try-on.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:16.031
<v The Heroine>And the full rulebook — the three moves,
the check patterns, all of it — is

00:15:16.031 --> 00:15:22.650
<v The Heroine>shelved in the library, to keep and pull
down whenever an answer looks a little
too sure of itself.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:29.948
<v The Heroine>This week: take one real answer from an
AI tool — a meeting recap, a summary of

00:15:29.948 --> 00:15:36.170
<v The Heroine>a public page, a draft reply — and
verify three claims before it borrows
your name.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:40.590
<v The Heroine>One that leans on a name, a date, a
number, or a link.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:45.570
<v The Heroine>One that could be stale, or from the
wrong country, or the wrong client.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:51.243
<v The Heroine>And one you'd be embarrassed to say out
loud if someone asked, "where did that

00:15:51.243 --> 00:15:57.190
<v The Heroine>come from?" You need ten minutes and
enough self-respect not to let Chutney
handle the timeline.

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:03.510
<v The Heroine>A corkboard and trench coat are
optional... though honestly, that sounds
kind of fun.

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:10.497
<v The Heroine>Then, if you're feeling it: take the pop
quiz at SUNNYVAiLE High — ten questions

